[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for loading a **single-file PMTiles archive** (vector, raster or raster-DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`) that knows how to do the HTTP Range requests required by the PMTiles format.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the `pmtiles://` protocol **once**, before any map is created

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it with MapLibre (global registry)
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why this matters** – In MapLibre GL JS v4+ the `addProtocol` handler must return an object with a `data` property (`{data, cacheControl?, expires?}`). The `pmtilesProtocol.tile` shim already does that for you on all supported MapLibre versions (v3-v6). If you write your own handler you must follow the same contract.

---

## 3️⃣ Define a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (the most common case)

```json
{
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles"
}
```

### Raster imagery

```json
{
  "type": "raster",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/satellite.pmtiles",
  "tileSize": 256
}
```

### Raster-DEM (terrain)

```json
{
  "type": "raster-dem",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/terrain.pmtiles",
  "encoding": "terrarium"   // or "mapbox"
}
```

> **Important:** Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`).  
> The PMTiles protocol reads the archive header, extracts the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`/`bounds`, and returns a TileJSON to MapLibre. If you use a `tiles:` template you lose that automatic zoom-range handling and MapLibre will assume a default `maxzoom: 22`, causing unnecessary empty-tile requests.

---

## 4️⃣ Put the source into a style and add layers

```js
const style = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    // ← the source we just defined
    world: {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
    }
  },
  layers: [
    {
      id: 'water',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'world',
      'source-layer': 'water',          // name of the vector layer inside the PMTiles file
      paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
    },
    {
      id: 'roads',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'world',
      'source-layer': 'road',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#777',
        'line-width': 1
      }
    }
    // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own `source-layer`
  ]
};
```

If you are using a raster-DEM source you also need to tell the style to use it for terrain:

```json
"terrain": {
  "source": "elevation",
  "exaggeration": 1.5
}
```

---

## 5️⃣ Create the map

```js
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div>
  style: style,              // the object from step 4
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

That’s it – the map will request only the byte ranges it needs from the `.pmtiles` file, and the client will render the vector (or raster) data just like any other tile source.

---

## 6️⃣ Hosting requirements (quick checklist)

| Requirement | What to do |
|-------------|------------|
| **HTTP Range support** | Any static host (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.) that returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| **CORS** | The host must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and allow the `Range` header. |
| **Cache headers** | For immutable archives set a long `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages caps at 100 MiB; other providers usually allow many GBs. Split into multiple PMTiles files if you exceed the limit. |

---

## 7️⃣ Optional: Using PMTiles in a React app

```jsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

export default function MyMap() {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Register once when the app starts
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: { /* style object from step 4 */ },
      center: [0, 0],
      zoom: 2
    });

    return () => {
      map.remove();
      // Optional cleanup if you ever unload the whole app
      maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
    };
  }, []);

  return <div id="map" style={{ width: '100%', height: '100vh' }} />;
}
```

Only the **first** call to `addProtocol` matters – the registry is global, so you don’t need to repeat it for every map component.

---

## 8️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Tiles appear blank (vector) or missing (raster) | Source defined with `tiles:` template instead of `url:` | Switch to `url:` as shown in step 3 |
| Console shows `Range header not allowed` | CORS on the host does not allow `Range` | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range` (or `*`) and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` |
| “Tile fetch error: 404” | Wrong URL or the file isn’t publicly readable | Verify the URL works in a browser (you should be able to download the whole file) |
| “Tile data is empty” after upgrading MapLibre | Custom protocol returns a raw `ArrayBuffer` instead of `{data: …}` | Use `pmtilesProtocol.tile` (already shimmed) or return `{data: buffer}` from your own handler |
| Very large file (> 100 MiB) on GitHub Pages | GitHub Pages limit | Host on S3/R2/Cloudflare or split the archive with `pmtiles extract` |

---

## 9️⃣ TL;DR – Minimal working example (plain HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
