Open the page, and you’ll see the vector data coming straight from the single `.pmtiles` file—no tile server required.

---

### 🎉 You’re done!

*You now have a static, server-less tile source that works everywhere MapLibre GL JS runs.* If you need to generate or inspect your own PMTiles archives, check the **PMTiles CLI** (`pmtiles convert`, `pmtiles show`, `pmtiles verify`, `pmtiles extract`) and tools like **tippecanoe** or **Planetiler**—but the client-side integration is exactly what’s shown above. Happy mapping!

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler now receives an **`AbortController`** instead of a callback.  
The handler **must resolve to an object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
If you return a bare `ArrayBuffer`, `Blob`, `Uint8Array`, or `Response`, MapLibre will treat the tile as “no data” and you’ll see blank tiles (or a silent failure).

```js
// v4+ signature
maplibregl.addProtocol(
  'mytiles',
  async (params, abortController) => {
    // …fetch tile, respect abortController.signal…
    return { data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob|Response> };
  }
);
```

Because of that signature change, any code that still uses the old callback form will stop working after the upgrade.

---

## Quick fix for a custom tile source

### 1️⃣ Convert the old callback handler to the new async-return-object form

```js
// BEFORE (v3)
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

```js
// AFTER (v4+)
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
  // Respect aborts – the browser may cancel the request when the map moves fast
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the tile request and makes MapLibre show an error tile
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // You can return any of the following as `data`:
  //   - ArrayBuffer
  //   - Uint8Array
  //   - Blob
  //   - Response (the whole fetch response)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The required shape is { data: <your bytes>, cacheControl?, expires? }
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| What you must do | Why |
|------------------|-----|
| Return **`{ data: … }`** | MapLibre extracts the tile bytes from the `data` field. |
| Accept an **`AbortController`** as the second argument | Allows the request to be cancelled when the map no longer needs the tile. |
| Throw on non-OK responses (or return `{ data: null }`) | Guarantees the error propagates to the map’s error handling. |
| (Optional) Return `cacheControl` / `expires` | If you want to control HTTP-style caching for that tile. |

### 2️⃣ If you already use the **PMTiles** library, you don’t need to rewrite anything

The `pmtiles` package ships a **v3-compat shim** that automatically adapts to both the old callback style and the new promise style:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();   // creates the handler
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile); // works on v3, v4, v5, v6
```

So, for PMTiles-based sources you can keep the single line above and it will keep working after the upgrade.

---

## Full example – custom vector tiles hosted on a static server

Below is a minimal, self-contained example that works with MapLibre GL JS v4+ (and later). It shows the registration of a custom protocol, a style that references the protocol, and proper cleanup.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 custom protocol demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Register a custom protocol named "mytiles"
  // -------------------------------------------------
  maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
    // Example: tiles are stored as https://my.cdn.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
    const url = `https://my.cdn.com/tiles/${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y}.pbf`;

    const response = await fetch(url, { signal: abortController.signal });

    if (!response.ok) {
      // MapLibre will treat this as a tile error (red “X” in dev mode)
      throw new Error(`Tile ${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y} → ${response.status}`);
    }

    // Return the raw bytes in the required wrapper
    const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
    return { data: buffer };
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // The source uses the protocol we just registered
        mySource: {
          type: 'vector',
          // Note: use `url:` (not `tiles:`) so MapLibre can read the TileJSON
          // from the protocol handler. The protocol handler can also return
          // a TileJSON if you need minzoom/maxzoom, bounds, etc.
          url: 'mytiles://https://my.cdn.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'water',   // name of the layer inside the vector tiles
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Clean-up (optional but good practice)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // If you ever destroy the map (e.g. in a SPA), remove the protocol
  // so you don’t leak the handler.
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('mytiles');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the example demonstrates

| Feature | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------|
| **`addProtocol` receives `(params, abortController)`** | Matches the v4+ signature. |
| **Returns `{ data: buffer }`** | The only shape MapLibre recognises. |
| **Uses `url:` (not `tiles:`)** | Lets the protocol read the TileJSON header (min/max zoom, bounds) from the archive or from a custom JSON you could return. |
| **`removeProtocol` on map teardown** | Prevents memory leaks in long-running apps (React, Vue, etc.). |
| **Works with PMTiles** | If you replace the handler with `new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`, the same registration line works unchanged. |

---

## Adapting an existing callback-style handler (step-by-step)

If you have a more complex handler that currently looks like this:

```js
function oldHandler(params, callback) {
  // maybe you do some custom auth, logging, etc.
  const url = buildUrl(params);
  fetch(url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
}
maplibregl.addProtocol('custom', oldHandler);
```

### Convert it

```js
async function newHandler(params, abortController) {
  // Keep your custom logic (auth headers, logging, etc.)
  const url = buildUrl(params);

  // Example of adding an Authorization header
  const headers = new Headers();
  headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${myAccessToken}`);

  const response = await fetch(url, {
    method: 'GET',
    headers,
    signal: abortController.signal   // <-- important for cancellation
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Propagate the error to MapLibre
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed (${response.status})`);
  }

  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // Return the required wrapper
  return { data: buffer };
}

maplibregl.addProtocol('custom', newHandler);
```

**Things to double-check**

1. **Abort handling** – If you ignore `abortController.signal`, the request will continue even after the map no longer needs the tile, wasting bandwidth.
2. **Error propagation** – Throw an `Error` (or return a rejected Promise). Do **not** call `callback(err)` because the callback no longer exists.
3. **Return shape** – Always return an object with a `data` key. If you also have HTTP-style caching info you can add `cacheControl` and `expires`:

   ```js
   return {
     data: buffer,
     cacheControl: 'public, max-age=31536000',
     expires: new Date(Date.now() + 365 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000).toUTCString()
   };
   ```

---

## When to keep the old code (and why you probably shouldn’t)

*If you are still on MapLibre GL JS v3* you can keep the callback version, but as soon as you upgrade any part of your stack (e.g., a dependency that pulls in v4) the handler will break.  
Because the library is now at v5+ (and v6 is already released), **sticking with the old API is a technical debt** that will bite you again when you upgrade other packages.

**Recommendation:** migrate all custom protocol handlers to the new async-return-object form now. It’s only a few lines of change and future-proofs your code.

---

## TL;DR checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|----|--------|
| 1️⃣ | Change `addProtocol` handler signature to `async (params, abortController) => { … }`. |
| 2️⃣ | Inside the handler, `await fetch(..., { signal: abortController.signal })`. |
| 3️⃣ | Throw on non-OK responses (or reject the promise). |
| 4️⃣ | Return **`{ data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob|Response> }`** (the `data` wrapper is mandatory). |
| 5️⃣ | If you use PMTiles, replace your custom handler with `new pmtiles.Protocol().tile` – it already implements the v4 shim. |
| 6️⃣ | Register the protocol **once**, before any `new maplibregl.Map()` is created. |
| 7️⃣ | (Optional) Call `maplibregl.removeProtocol('yourProtocol')` when the last map is destroyed. |
| 8️⃣ | Verify that the host serving the tiles supports **HTTP Range** and sends proper **CORS** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`). |
| 9️⃣ | Test with the browser dev tools → Network → check that a request for `.../0/0/0.pbf` returns a `206 Partial Content` (range) and that the response body is not empty. |
| 🔟 | If you still see blank tiles, make sure you are using `url:` (not `tiles:`) in the source definition, so MapLibre can read the TileJSON header and know the correct zoom

**Short answer:** No – PMTiles is a *static* archive. It’s perfect when you want to serve a fixed set of tiles (basemaps, historic data, pre-generated raster imagery, etc.) without running any server-side code. For a live feed that changes every 30 seconds you need a source that can be queried on-the-fly (e.g. a tile server that reads from PostGIS or a simple GeoJSON/FeatureCollection that you poll).

Below is a quick decision matrix, then a concrete recommendation for your bus-position use-case, and a few code snippets to get you started.

---

## 1️⃣ Why PMTiles isn’t a good fit for “live” data

| Characteristic | PMTiles | Dynamic tile source (PostGIS-backed) |
|----------------|---------|--------------------------------------|
| **Data mutability** | Immutable after the file is written. To change a single tile you must rewrite the whole archive (or at least the affected tile-range) and re-upload the file. | Reads directly from the database each request, so any row change is reflected immediately (subject to cache headers). |
| **Update frequency** | Designed for *once-off* or *infrequent* releases (e.g. nightly basemap builds). | Handles sub-second to minute-scale updates without re-publishing a new file. |
| **Hosting** | Any static host that supports HTTP Range (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, CDN). | Needs a server that can execute SQL and return TileJSON/VectorTile or GeoJSON (e.g. pg_tileserv, t-rex, Martin, tileserver-gl, custom Express/Go service). |
| **Cache-ability** | You can set a very long `Cache-Control` because the content never changes. | You usually set a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `max-age=30` or `no-cache`) so the client fetches fresh data each poll. |
| **Complexity** | Zero-ops after upload – no backend to maintain. | Requires a small backend, but many ready-made services exist and they are lightweight. |

Because your bus positions change **every 30 seconds**, you would have to rebuild and re-upload a whole PMTiles file that often – which defeats the whole purpose of a static, server-less workflow and would be extremely wasteful in bandwidth and latency.

---

## 2️⃣ Recommended approaches for “live” bus positions

### A. Use a **GeoJSON source** with periodic polling

If the number of buses is modest (a few hundred at most) you can simply query the database (or an API that wraps it) and return a GeoJSON FeatureCollection. MapLibre can refresh the source on a timer.

**Pros**
- Very easy to set up (no tile server needed).
- Works well for point data that isn’t too dense.
- You can style each bus with `icon-image`, `text-field`, etc.

**Cons**
- Not ideal for very large point clouds (thousands+ per view) because the whole GeoJSON payload is transferred each poll.

**Example (plain JS):**

```html
<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>
<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/+esm';
import 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // placeholder – will be replaced by `setData`
      buses: {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] }
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'bus-points',
        type: 'circle',
        source: 'buses',
        paint: {
          'circle-radius': 6,
          'circle-color': '#ff6600',
          'circle-stroke-width': 1,
          'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Function that fetches the latest bus positions from your API
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
async function fetchBuses() {
  const resp = await fetch('https://my-api.example.com/bus-positions'); // returns GeoJSON
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${resp.status}`);
  const geojson = await resp.json();
  // Replace the source data – MapLibre will diff and repaint efficiently
  map.getSource('buses').setData(geojson);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Poll every 30 seconds
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
function startPolling() {
  fetchBuses().catch(console.error); // initial load
  setInterval(() => {
    fetchBuses().catch(console.error);
  }, 30_000);
}

// Wait for the map to be ready before touching sources
map.on('load', startPolling);
</script>
```

**Server side tip:** Expose a tiny HTTP endpoint that runs a simple SQL query like:

```sql
SELECT jsonb_build_object(
  'type', 'FeatureCollection',
  'features', jsonb_agg(
    jsonb_build_object(
      'type', 'Feature',
      'geometry', ST_AsGeoJSON(geom)::jsonb,
      'properties', jsonb_build_object(
        'id', bus_id,
        'route', route_name,
        'speed', speed_kph
      )
    )
  )
) AS geojson
FROM bus_positions
WHERE updated_at > now() - interval '1 minute';
```

If you’re using Node/Express, `pg` can stream the result directly to `res.json()`.

---

### B. Use a **vector-tile server** that reads from PostGIS

If you have **many** buses (tens of thousands) or you want to take advantage of tile-level clipping, you can serve **MVT** tiles on demand. Several lightweight, production-ready servers exist:

| Server | Language | How to point at PostGIS | Typical command |
|--------|----------|------------------------|-----------------|
| **pg_tileserv** | Go | `DATABASE_URL=postgres://...` | `pg_tileserv` (runs on :7800) |
| **t-rex** | Rust | `datasource = { dbname = "mydb", ... }` | `t-rex serve` |
| **Martin** (MapLibre’s own) | Go | `--postgis "host=… dbname=…"` | `martin serve` |
| **tileserver-gl** (Node) | Node | `postgis` plugin or custom script | `tileserver-gl config.json` |

All of these expose a **TileJSON** endpoint (`/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`) that MapLibre can consume just like any other vector source.

**Why this works better than PMTiles for live data**

- The server runs a fresh SQL query for each tile request, so any row change is reflected instantly.
- Tile caching can still be used (e.g., set `Cache-Control: max-age=30`), but you keep the ability to update frequently.
- You can still combine static basemap PMTiles (for streets, terrain, etc.) with a live vector-tile source for the buses.

**Example style snippet (using pg_tileserv):**

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://example.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles"
    },
    "buses": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": ["https://tiles.mydomain.com/bus/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 16,
      "attribution": "City Transit Authority"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "bus-points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "buses",
      "source-layer": "bus_positions",   // name defined in your SQL view / TileJSON
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 5,
        "circle-color": "#ff6600",
        "circle-stroke-width": 1,
        "circle-stroke-color": "#fff"
      }
    }
    // … other layers (roads, labels) from the PMTiles basemap …
  ]
}
```

**Important notes**

1. **CORS** – The tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) because the browser will request tiles cross-origin.
2. **Cache-Control** – Set a short max-age (e.g., `Cache-Control: public, max-age=30, stale-while-revalidate=60`) so browsers/CDNs don’t hold stale bus positions for too long.
3. **Protocol registration** – You still need the `pmtiles` protocol for the static basemap, but you **don’t** need it for the live vector source (it uses normal HTTP URLs).

---

## 3️⃣ When you *might* still want PMTiles in the same app

You can **mix** static PMTiles basemaps with a live source:

- **Basemap** (roads, landuse, terrain) → PMTiles (served from S3/CDN, cached forever).
- **Dynamic overlay** (bus positions) → pg_tileserv / t-rex / GeoJSON polling.

This gives you the best of both worlds: ultra-fast, cheap static tiles for the heavy background layers, and a lightweight dynamic layer for the moving objects.

---

## 4️⃣ TL;DR checklist for your bus-position map

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| ❌ | Do **not** generate a new PMTiles file every 30 s. |
| ✅ | Deploy a tiny vector-tile server (pg_tileserv, t-rex, Martin, or tileserver-gl) that reads the `bus_positions` table/view from PostGIS. |
| ✅ | In your MapLibre style, add a `vector` source that points to that server (`tiles: ["https://…/bus/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"]`). |
| ✅ | Set `Cache-Control: max-age=30` (or similar) on the tile server responses. |
| ✅ | (Optional) Keep your static basemap in PMTiles and register the `pmtiles` protocol once at app start. |
| ✅ | If the bus count is low (< ~500) and you prefer simplicity, you can skip the tile server and use a `geojson` source with a 30-second poll. |

---

### Quick “starter” script for a Node-based tile server (pg_tileserv)

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install pg_tileserv (requires Go)
go install github.com/CrunchyData/pg_tileserv@latest   # puts binary in $GOPATH/bin

# 2️⃣ Set env vars (adjust for your DB)
export DATABASE_URL="postgres://user:pass@db-host:5432/transport"
export PG_TILESERV_PORT=7800
export PG_TILESERV_MAX_AGE=30   # seconds

# 3️⃣ Run
pg_tileserv
```

Now `http://localhost:7800/public.bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` will serve MVT tiles for the `public.bus_positions` view/table. Point your MapLibre source at that URL (or proxy it behind a CDN if you need global latency).

